00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.420
So Compute Engine-- these
are virtual machines.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.720
They are logically
computers, the same kinds

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.430
you'd plug-in and
use other places,

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.610
but running in Google Cloud.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.330
And you can have really quite
large computers these days.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:14.730
You know, lots of CPUs,
lots of memory, lots of disk

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:19.320
attached, lots of graphics
processing your GPUs

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.720
and sensor processing units
if you're doing machine

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.885
learning for this stuff.

00:03:23.885 --> 00:03:26.260
And one of the things that's
really interesting, I think,

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.968
about our VMs is that
all of these pieces

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:29.770
are individually configurable.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.460
So if you have a workload
that is very memory intensive

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.890
but doesn't do much
compute, or the opposite,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.040
or needs a huge amount
of storage space,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.500
you can actually make a
machine that's that size

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.170
and save you a bunch of
money, versus just making

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:44.922
a huge, large computer.

00:03:44.922 --> 00:03:45.630
So that's useful.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.140
They start quickly for virtual
machines, like around tens

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:49.640
of seconds.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.861
So 10, 20 seconds per machine.

00:03:51.861 --> 00:03:52.860
And these are computers.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.580
They're running a
whole operating system.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.870
So we have images built for
various Linux operating systems

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.810
and then also Windows
operating systems.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.560
And you can create
your own image

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.010
if you'd like to run something
very specific, perhaps

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.550
a company image or
things like that.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:09.990
Diving into disk
just little bit,

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.510
because I think it's
an interesting example,

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:16.529
and they're true
throughout our compute.

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.320
But disk kind of
really makes it shine.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.770
Our default disks are all
network attached storage.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.740
So they're are
collections of pieces

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.170
of disk, parts of
thousands of physical disks

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:29.910
across a whole data center.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:33.907
And so when you make
one larger, your disk

00:04:33.907 --> 00:04:35.490
just gets faster and
faster and faster

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.050
as you use more and
more of the data center.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:41.010
And it allows you to take
snapshots live, copy those

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.260
to another machine, make a
new computer out of them,

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.170
maybe in another part
of the world, easy

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:47.410
backups, that sort of thing.

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:51.180
So as you're
thinking about these,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.340
like virtual machines
feel like computers,

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.816
but every aspect of it
is at data center scale.

00:04:57.816 --> 00:04:59.940
So your disks, your
networking, your load balancer,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.680
all this stuff.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.420
So I guess try to
keep an open mind

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.670
as you're evaluating the
different pieces, because you

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.503
can make exactly the
size computer you want,

00:05:07.503 --> 00:05:10.860
make it as fast as you need,
and change these on the fly,

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.960
including changing the
disk size without rebooting

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.695
the machine, which is handy
if anybody's ever run out

00:05:15.695 --> 00:05:19.770
of disk before, which I've done.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.050
And kind of more than
just virtual machines.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:26.670
So if you make a machine
that you really like and you

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:30.720
want to have sibling machines
that do exactly the same work,

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.060
you can basically kind
of take that image

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.920
and make a managed
instance group

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.140
and say, OK, I'd like to
have five of those now.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.240
Now I'd like 10,
back down to three.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.560
And you can just have Compute
Engine just scale up and down.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.220
And you can even do that
automatically based on load.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.030
And just a moment to talk
about our load balancer--

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.530
if you want to dig
into the details,

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:55.410
there's a paper on
it called Maglev.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.120
The internal name
is called Maglev.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.139
And our load
balancers that you're

00:05:59.139 --> 00:06:00.930
using, from Compute
Engine and all the rest

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:02.721
the products I'm going
to talk about today,

00:06:02.721 --> 00:06:04.950
are actually the data
center scale load balancers

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.140
that we use to run
all of Google off of.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.457
So when you're making a
configuration change here,

00:06:09.457 --> 00:06:11.290
it's the same load
balancing infrastructure,

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.770
which means it scales up
and down incredibly quickly.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:17.160
You can make a new load
balancer, and within about

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.370
a minute, hit it with a million
requests per second of traffic

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:24.630
and then drop down to
nothing, and it works.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.830
So with you as a developer,
as a project manager,

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:32.280
as a team, when you're
using virtual machines

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.170
you're thinking
about your software,

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:37.710
the shape of the computer,
the operating system, what

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.270
the networking connections are.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.510
It's very similar to working
with on premise hardware,

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.400
in a way.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:47.690
But just be careful,
because it's all software.

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:52.000
But you're responsible for
all those same concerns.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.442
So this is a really good
fit for lift and shift kind

00:06:54.442 --> 00:06:56.400
of scenarios, where you
have existing software,

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.510
you want to bring it over.

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:00.210
A non-obvious one,
if you just want

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:04.290
to run one containerized process
on a computer of arbitrary

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.750
size, that's really easy
to do with Compute Engine.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:10.950
You can point it at an image
and boot it up and have it go.

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:14.520
If you have a need for a really
particular version of a Linux

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.070
kernel or if you have
very specific licensing

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.260
requirements, this
can be the good,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.180
or perhaps, only fit to do this.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.270
It's great for running
databases and the like.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:31.530
And you can do nearly all
of the network protocols.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:35.420
UDP broadcast we don't do, but--

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:38.840
On the constraint side, it
takes about 10 to 20 seconds

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:40.160
to start up one machine.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.890
You can start up thousands
in around a minute.

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:45.350
That's a pretty fast
response, but some loads

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.060
need faster response time.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.220
So that might be a constraint.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:54.980
And it's completely up to you
to manage the whole software

00:07:54.980 --> 00:08:00.750
stack, which is important
to recognize, you know,

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.864
how you update your
software, and the like.

00:08:03.864 --> 00:08:05.030
So what does this feel like?

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:07.100
I've been talking
for too long already.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:07.610
Let's go.

