00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:40.630
I need to have this generation
or newer of CPUs, and the like.

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:43.780
And those do vary a bit by zone.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:47.830
And we can, in here, choose
what kind of disk it's on.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:50.070
Any of the kind of
pre-built things,

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.140
various linuxes,
different versions

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:56.500
of Windows Server and
the like, or your own,

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.960
and all kinds of
other fun stuff.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.570
But I'm going to go ahead and--
oh, right before I hit Create.

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:03.740
Down here at the
bottom-- so when

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:05.174
you're learning
something new, you

00:10:05.174 --> 00:10:06.340
want to be exploring, right?

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:08.140
So the web UI is
really good for that.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:10.737
Once you figure
out what you want,

00:10:10.737 --> 00:10:12.570
if you do it through
there, you can actually

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:16.470
click down here and get either
the exact kind of JSON payload

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:20.250
to pass to our API, or the
command line that would

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:22.780
create exactly the same image.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:26.560
So you can then run this from
G Cloud and get the same thing.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:27.990
So if I hit the Create button--

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:31.284
and we'll start a
mental timer going of,

00:10:31.284 --> 00:10:32.700
you know, right
now we've actually

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:35.130
