00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.630
and then it's going to connect
to me via the web over,

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:08.536
you know, to SSH
into the machine

00:11:08.536 --> 00:11:09.660
and actually do work on it.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:17.200
So we can take a look and we
can have some scrolling text.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:20.360
Oh, I have to do that as pseudo.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:21.030
It's real.

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:21.988
I got an error message.

00:11:24.039 --> 00:11:25.580
And so we have
scrolling text proving

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:27.140
that it's a real computer.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:30.560
Oh, bounce.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.310
So that's a quick taste
of Compute Engine.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.220
There's a whole lot there.

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.790
Please take a look.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.960
So why might you
choose Compute Engine?

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.190
It is very consistent.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:44.720
If you do your testing on
10 VMs in a given zone,

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.570
and later you want to use those
same 10 VMs somewhere else,

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:51.195
you should get very similar,
almost the identical behavior,

00:11:51.195 --> 00:11:53.070
wherever you run them,
whatever you run them.

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:54.560
So it should be very consistent.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:57.970
These custom sizes,
live disk resize.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:00.020
In other talks they
probably talked in detail,

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:01.700
but basically, if you
use the same shape

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.946
instance for a long
time they get cheaper

00:12:03.946 --> 00:12:05.070
over the course of a month.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:07.640
So sustained use
discounts automatically

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:08.990
if you leave that going.

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:11.660
If you have batch
jobs, we have something

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:13.340
called preemptible VMs.

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:14.870
So they're VMs
that are guaranteed

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:18.230
not to live more than 24 hours,
might shut off at any time.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:22.410
But they're dramatically
less expensive to run.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:25.146
And behind the
scenes, if we were

00:12:25.146 --> 00:12:27.020
planning to do any work
on the host machines,

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.390
we actually move the
VMs to another computer

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:30.980
without dropping
network connections,

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:34.540
usually within
milliseconds to do that.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:37.190
And it's naturally a good fit
for almost all the software

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:38.320
that's out there currently.

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.486
It's running on computers
with networks between them

00:12:40.486 --> 00:12:42.110
and that sort of thing.

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:44.611
And by the way, we do
run services on top of GC

00:12:44.611 --> 00:12:45.110
ourselves.

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:53.780
So Kubernetes Engine, our Cloud
SQL instances, and other things

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:55.790
are actually running on
top of Compute Engine.

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:57.790
So a lot of what we're
going to talk about later

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:00.810
inherits all of these qualities
that we just talked about.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:02.990
So head to the docs--

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:05.540
all sorts of information
about Compute Engine there.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:08.120
And there are quite
a lot of talks,

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:10.220
but here's a few selected
talks to check out

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:13.040
for more details
about Compute Engine.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:20.010
So photo time, and done.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:24.240
OK, so Kubernetes Engine,
as you might guess,

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.790
is Kubernetes hosted
on Google Cloud.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:32.070
So anybody heard of containers?

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:33.864
Thank you for the laugh.

00:13:33.864 --> 00:13:36.030
So how many people have
heard of Kubernetes, though?

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.470
I'd like to get a sense.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.000
So like, kind of heard the word.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.208
How many people are using
Kubernetes in some fashion,

00:13:41.208 --> 00:13:42.240
anywhere?

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.610
OK, quite a lot, maybe 10%, 20%.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:45.300
That's awesome.

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.280
Thank you.

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:51.245
So the quick version of kind
of why containers, in my mind.

00:13:51.245 --> 00:13:52.620
Have you ever
heard somebody say,

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:55.580
well, it works on my machine.

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:57.770
I mean, containers basically
solve that problem.

00:13:57.770 --> 00:13:59.370
It actually works
in all the places,

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:03.450
because it has the exact same
binary and the same file system

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:05.706
that you use to test
and to validate,

00:14:05.706 --> 00:14:07.580
and you bring that to
production and bring it

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:08.496
to the other machines.

00:14:08.496 --> 00:14:13.350
So in a short, that's my
personal feeling there.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:16.600
Kubernetes comes into play when
you have lots of containers.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:19.470
And you want to think
about multiple computers

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:22.600
and large numbers of
containers as one system,

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:24.600
and reason about it in that way.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:29.520
So it is inspired by systems
built previously at Google,

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:32.310
and built in the open, like
released in public, you know,

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:36.810
at a point one release, and got
dramatically better because we

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:39.065
worked with real use cases
with real users, lots

00:14:39.065 --> 00:14:41.190
of contributions from a
lot of different companies,

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:42.660
a lot of individuals.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:46.200
Just crossed over
20,000 contributors.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.950
The Cloud Native Compute
Foundation owns all the IP.

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:52.440
It is 100 percent open
source, and at this point,

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:54.160
runs pretty much everywhere.

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:58.230
So it's worth looking
into, in my opinion.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.240
But conceptually,
you're thinking

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:04.770
more about applications
than computers,

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:06.660
so logical infrastructure.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.887
And the short
version is, you know,

00:15:08.887 --> 00:15:10.470
when you get together
with a colleague

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:12.511
and you're trying to
explain how something works,

00:15:12.511 --> 00:15:14.900
you immediately start drawing
things on the white board--

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:18.450
Kubernetes is not far from
writing config files that look

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:19.890
like those lines and boxes.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.700
I mean, it's in YAML and
text, so it's more fiddly.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:27.210
But conceptually, you're
connecting the application

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:30.720
to the database to the backend
service that like, runs.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:33.090
And you're actually
defining those connections

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:36.600
in config and telling
Kubernetes to be

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.160
responsible for
making that continue

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:41.540
to work, which is awesome.

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.610
And then Kubernetes Engine
is hosted Kubernetes.

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:48.390
So there is some effort involved
in kind of running a cluster

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:49.409
and keeping it up to--

00:15:49.409 --> 00:15:51.450
working to getting one
started for the first time

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:54.480
to like, evaluate whether you
even care about Kubernetes.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.100
So if you're doing
an evaluation,

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:58.016
it really makes a lot
of sense, in my opinion,

00:15:58.016 --> 00:15:59.776
to go straight to
Kubernetes Engine.

00:15:59.776 --> 00:16:01.650
You can get one running
in about five minutes

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:04.620
and see if it works for you
in the projects you have,

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:05.970
and go from there.

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:09.540
And basically, it keeps the
individual computer nodes up

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:10.440
to date.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:13.015
It updates Kubernetes
itself, helps you

00:16:13.015 --> 00:16:15.390
with auto scaling and things
like that, and a whole bunch

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:17.550
more now.

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:20.790
So this is a really
good fit if you

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:22.862
are running multiple
environments of any kind

00:16:22.862 --> 00:16:24.570
and you need to kind
of insulate yourself

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:26.260
from the differences
between them.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:31.020
And that can be multiple clouds,
it can be in your own machines

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:34.380
with a cloud, it can be
different dev test prod

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:37.230
environments that you
want to behave the same

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:40.800
but are actually
isolated from each other,

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:41.910
those kinds of things.

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:46.920
It does need, in my
opinion here, again,

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:48.060
good team communication.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.572
Because we're actually mixing
some of the responsibilities

00:16:50.572 --> 00:16:52.530
that have been traditionally
kind of separated.

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:54.540
You know, security
folks, the people

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:56.264
who own the computers,
the people who

00:16:56.264 --> 00:16:57.930
are responsible for
the software running

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.330
on them, all of those pieces.

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:02.010
They need to have
good communication.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:05.880
You're probably much better
off if you have source control

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:09.384
with config and your
application data,

00:17:09.384 --> 00:17:10.800
you know, the apps
that you write,

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:12.930
and go through some sort of
build process so that everyone

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:15.054
who makes changes, they go
through the same process

00:17:15.054 --> 00:17:18.486
and end up in a test
environment later production.

00:17:18.486 --> 00:17:23.230
It kind of implies it
works much better that way.

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:25.920
One big constraint is
Kubernetes only runs containers.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:28.260
So you do need to get your
software kind of wrapped up

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:30.360
in a container.

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:33.990
There can be some fiddly
bits around licensing.

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:37.470
Some software is
licensed per physical CPU

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:38.544
that it ever runs on.

00:17:38.544 --> 00:17:39.960
So you need to pay
extra attention

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:43.800
to not run it in too many
computers, things like that.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.650
And you know, maybe some
architectural details

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:49.530
if you've got an app that
has really particular needs

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:51.990
about how the pieces of it
communicate to each other.

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.880
Might not be a perfect
fit in that case.

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:59.430
What this looks
like in practice--

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:02.010
over here on Kubernetes
Engine, I actually

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:05.250
have, in cooking show style,
I have a cluster already

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:10.200
deployed, and I have set up
a service that is listening

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:13.290
to, right now, nothing.

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:17.370
So you click on it and
you get nothing back.

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:22.230
And if we go in here,
this is a kind of Google--

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:25.290
you know, the Kubernetes
Engine UI for managing

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:27.060
things in a cluster.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.160
You can also do this all
from the command line,

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:30.690
and same story--

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:33.450
you can get kind of
the raw config file

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:36.390
for what we do in the
UI, so that you can

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:39.690
do that programmatically later.

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:45.510
And I'm going to create
a new set of pods, which

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:48.240
are a collection
of processes that

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.170
have their own kind
of-- they're doing work.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:51.990
And I'm going to like,
use an image that's

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:53.610
already built called nginx.

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:56.220
And this is a web server that
is very, very popular, runs

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:57.220
very well.

00:18:57.220 --> 00:19:00.360
So this will be just a default
deployment of a web server.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:04.280
And I'm going to leave all
the other stuff default

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:05.970
and click Deploy here.

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:08.790
And basically, it's kind of
making some decisions for me,

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:13.254
like configuring an auto scaler
and kind of a default number.

00:19:13.254 --> 00:19:15.420
We start with three copies
of this running somewhere

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:19.620
in our cluster, and then
depending upon CPU load,

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:22.860
we'll actually deploy more
or fewer pods over time.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:27.000
And this adjusts pretty rapidly,
depending on how much usage

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:27.930
it's actually getting.

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:30.600
And so that can be based on
CPU or a lot of other things

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:33.840
to actually scale the pods up
and down inside of the cluster

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:35.790
that is already running.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:40.350
And then separately, cluster
size can also be scaled.

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:43.440
So now, if we come
back to services,

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:45.570
click on this same
thing, we have,

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:48.840
ta-da, a brand new nginx thing.

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:51.560
Isn't that amazing?

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:56.530
But that's a really
quick kind of intro

00:19:56.530 --> 00:19:58.510
that I think the main
takeaway there is,

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:01.240
once you have the
cluster up, you

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:04.170
tell Kubernetes what state
you want the world to be in,

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:06.170
and it's its job to
keep it that way.

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:08.650
So if one of these
processes dies,

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:10.090
Kubernetes will restart it.

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:12.290
If one of the computers
that one of the nodes

00:20:12.290 --> 00:20:15.190
that Kubernetes cluster
is made up of goes away,

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:17.050
it will be restarted.

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:21.577
And basically, that lets us all
kind of sleep better at night,

00:20:21.577 --> 00:20:22.910
is that is the main thing there.

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:26.530
So let's go back to the talk.

00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:29.437
So you might choose Kubernetes.

00:20:29.437 --> 00:20:31.270
Like I said before, it
kind of insulates you

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:33.640
from changes of
different environments.

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:36.430
It gives you kind of a higher
level of abstraction to reason

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:38.605
about, but still lets you
do almost all the things

00:20:38.605 --> 00:20:39.730
you want to do in software.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:44.320
One of the main
reasons Google invested

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:47.290
in this kind of technology
is to save money, actually.

00:20:47.290 --> 00:20:50.500
So it enables you to
pack a lot more software

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:53.950
together on the same computers,
without messing each other up.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:56.200
Because you have the
isolation from containers,

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:58.990
you can specify how much CPU
and memory you want each of them

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:01.496
to use, and they
scale up and down

00:21:01.496 --> 00:21:02.620
within the set of machines.

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:05.562
So instead of having computers
kind of sitting aside in case

00:21:05.562 --> 00:21:07.270
you get traffic to
them, you can actually

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:09.103
have multiple different
applications running

00:21:09.103 --> 00:21:12.010
in the same space and
scale up and down as needed

00:21:12.010 --> 00:21:13.797
and save a bunch of
money, especially

00:21:13.797 --> 00:21:15.880
if you're running in cloud,
where you can actually

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:17.530
change the number
of nodes that you're

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:20.200
paying for at the same time.

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:22.840
And the distraction level, the
white boarding kind of level,

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:25.240
is a real sweet spot for
a lot of communication

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:29.470
and for a lot of reasoning
about your systems.

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:31.000
You might choose
Kubernetes Engine,

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:32.740
like I said, because
it's managed,

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:34.890
lets you focus on it
a bit more, very quick

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:37.430
start, automatic
updates, good stuff.

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:40.960
So again, check
out the docs first

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:43.115
and there are
multiple talks here.

00:21:43.115 --> 00:21:44.740
This is actually only
a small fraction.

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:46.115
I think we have
something like 20

00:21:46.115 --> 00:21:49.750
or 30 Kubernetes related
talks at the event.

00:21:49.750 --> 00:21:53.000
And some of the things from
yesterday are already online.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:57.200
So check those out and send
them to friends, colleagues,

00:21:57.200 --> 00:21:58.120
and the like.

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:00.220
And moving on.

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:06.150
So App Engine-- this is the
transition from infrastructure

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:08.360
into kind of
developer-focused code.

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:13.200
So how long can I avoid
saying serverless?

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:14.690
Let's see.

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:17.720
Oh, not very long.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:20.030
That actually wasn't
super intentional.

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:23.240
So App Engine primarily
is made and was

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:25.547
made to help your
developers, which

00:22:25.547 --> 00:22:29.930
would be you, focus on code
and just push that up and let

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:31.550
us handle the rest.

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:34.910
And its kind of sweet spot is
things that scale up and down

00:22:34.910 --> 00:22:37.424
really rapidly.

00:22:37.424 --> 00:22:38.840
It's perfectly
fine running things

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:41.330
with consistent workloads, but
it adapts very, very quickly

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:44.164
based on actual activity.

00:22:44.164 --> 00:22:46.580
Each of the process is actually
run inside of a container,

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:50.134
and so they spin up on our
infrastructure very quickly.

00:22:50.134 --> 00:22:51.800
It's actually been
around quite a while.

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:54.080
It started in 2008.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:56.295
At that time, and up
until fairly recently,

00:22:56.295 --> 00:22:57.920
there have been quite
a few constraints

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:01.010
around the kind of code you
could run on App Engine.

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:04.940
Had to be certain run times.

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:07.670
Only allowed certain libraries
within those languages

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:09.770
to be used.

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:12.620
Last year, we announced
the flexible environment,

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:14.660
which is basically
App Engine managed

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:18.500
VMs, kind of running containers
in those VMs, which enables you

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:20.300
to run a wide
variety of languages,

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:23.750
completely custom Docker
images and the like.

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:27.800
And just recently, like
yesterday, I think,

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:32.120
we announced several new second
generation run times that

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:35.030
are App Engine standard style.

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:41.195
But they are the same
language environment

00:23:41.195 --> 00:23:43.070
that you would get just
by downloading Python

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.720
or downloading Node.js, PHP.

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:47.360
We're starting
with three of them.

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:49.430
This is in the
"coming very soon,"

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:53.060
like next week or
so kind of frame.

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:57.510
And why this kind
of all of a sudden?

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:00.810
The old run times we used
to take a lot of effort

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:03.670
to secure them, make special
versions and the like.

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:08.010
And the key
difference is gVisor.

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:10.380
And this is a recently
open source project

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:17.220
that allows you to run untrusted
code safely, without a VM

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:18.670
abstraction in between.

00:24:18.670 --> 00:24:21.690
So it's basically
a syscall proxy

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:25.410
for applications on Linux,
and let's kind of safe calls

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:27.450
through to the kernel
and blocks other calls.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:29.580
And it's running in
user land, so it's

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:32.430
very robust in terms
of being independent

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:34.140
from attacks in the kernel.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:38.880
So gVisor and pieces around
this make us confident

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:40.890
that we can run,
essentially, arbitrary

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:42.950
code safely across our fleet.

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:46.380
And it's allowing us to
move much, much faster

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:49.740
in updating App
Engine, which leads us

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:53.380
to a slightly more complicated
space to reason about.

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:57.630
So hopefully, this
is a useful kind

00:24:57.630 --> 00:25:00.210
of breakdown of these three
different environments

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:01.860
and some of the
trade offs there.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:06.510
So this talk is a choose
with a choose inside of it.

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:11.796
But basically, the--

00:25:11.796 --> 00:25:12.803
I don't know.

00:25:12.803 --> 00:25:14.950
[? Maybe they ?] don't
make sense on its own.

00:25:14.950 --> 00:25:17.320
But the two to the
left, the standard ones,

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:19.330
are sandboxed
individual processes

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:21.910
running on [? serverless ?]
set of machines.

00:25:21.910 --> 00:25:25.090
Flexible is actually
managing virtual machines

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:26.740
in your project.

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:28.240
And you can write
an arbitrary code,

00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:31.120
you can resize those to whatever
size machine you would like,

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:32.680
that sort of thing.

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:37.900
The 2nd gen and
Flexible are using--

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:41.920
you basically use Google
Cloud APIs directly.

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:46.210
The original standard
run times, many of them

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:50.177
existed before the
rest of Cloud existed.

00:25:50.177 --> 00:25:51.760
And so they kind of
were incorporating

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:54.610
things that are now part of
Cloud normally, a blob store.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:57.250
We just recently
announced a scheduler,

00:25:57.250 --> 00:25:59.840
which is basically our crawl in
service, and things like that.

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:02.260
So all of those are available
to all of our execution

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:03.093
of environments now.

00:26:03.093 --> 00:26:05.660
So the new pieces
use those directly.

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:08.680
And the big deal for
developers, in many ways,

00:26:08.680 --> 00:26:13.450
is that you can run any Python
module, any kind of no JS

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:15.430
extension.

00:26:15.430 --> 00:26:17.930
And just write the
code the same way

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:19.430
you'd write it in
other environments

00:26:19.430 --> 00:26:20.388
and it should just run.

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:25.350
So when you're
working in App Engine,

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:27.890
you are thinking about
the code you're writing,

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:32.130
HTTP requests and responses,
and versions, which

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:34.340
I'll demo here in a second.

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:36.330
But basically, you have
a version of your code

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:37.440
and then you can have
a whole other version

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:39.780
of the application deployed
at the same time, which

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:40.490
is kind of cool.

00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:41.725
So let's take a look at that.

00:26:41.725 --> 00:26:45.280
Oh, before we do, the
constraints thing.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:51.600
So it is a really
good fit for anything

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.960
that is kind of HTTP
request and response based.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:58.530
Stateless applications are
particularly easy to scale.

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:01.740
And I talked to one earlier--
if you have applications

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:05.190
that scale very rapidly, perhaps
from almost zero to millions

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:09.720
of requests per minute or per
second and then back down,

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:12.390
that's a really good fit
for that sort of thing.

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:17.540
And actually, refer back
to this for constraints.

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:20.680
So demo-- so let's
take a look at this.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:24.990
So the App Engine dashboard
looks a bit like this.

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:30.020
And I have one app deployed.

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:33.440
And if we take a look at
it, all it does is return

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:36.400
an image of lemons.

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:39.710
This is an amazing application.

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:43.510
Thank you for laughing.

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:45.250
However, I have
gotten some feedback

00:27:45.250 --> 00:27:47.350
that not everyone
likes lemons, even

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:49.845
though I love the sour lemon.

00:27:49.845 --> 00:27:51.970
So we have actually created
some different versions

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:53.350
of the application.

00:27:53.350 --> 00:27:56.780
And they're all
deployed simultaneously.

00:27:56.780 --> 00:28:00.490
And we can actually kind of poke
at the serving URLs for them

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:01.010
directly.

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:04.270
So this is actually a
cherry dot, this site.

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:06.820
And so you can actually test
and look at and have people

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:09.961
evaluate, have scripts run
against different versions

00:28:09.961 --> 00:28:11.460
of the application
at the same time,

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:16.340
while the main one is
serving a previous version.

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:17.810
So that's pretty cool.

00:28:17.810 --> 00:28:19.240
And then when you decide--

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:20.620
actually, let's check here.

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:21.750
So we have options.

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:23.550
So lemon's the current state.

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:24.820
How many people like cherries?

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:29.930
Some cherries, or not.

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:32.330
How about apples?

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:33.710
More apples than cherries.

00:28:33.710 --> 00:28:35.880
And durian?

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:39.470
Oh, we have some durian lovers.

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:42.080
Just because, we're
going to go with durian.

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:45.320
So what we can do
is actually say

00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:47.450
we're going to ignore
popular opinion,

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:51.380
and I'm going to actually
move the site over

00:28:51.380 --> 00:28:54.350
to being 100% durian.

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:58.814
And then when we come back to
our main site and new users

00:28:58.814 --> 00:29:00.230
on the next request,
they're going

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:02.270
to get this version of the site.

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:05.090
So that's kind of a
straight cut over.

00:29:05.090 --> 00:29:07.580
But the really cool
thing is that we can then

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:08.900
cut back just as easily.

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:12.380
So I can come here and
say, you know, actually,

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:18.240
let's go confusingly 100% lemon.

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:21.860
Go back, maybe I will
leave this open next time.

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:25.400
And then when we refresh, we
get all lemons from there on.

00:29:25.400 --> 00:29:30.730
Or if you want to do a
percentage of your traffic,

00:29:30.730 --> 00:29:33.280
you want to test out your new
features without committing

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:37.090
to all of your users seeing
it, you can use the same tool

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:44.320
and basically say, actually,
we want to go just, say, 30%

00:29:44.320 --> 00:29:46.460
apples.

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:47.430
Hit Save there.

00:29:47.430 --> 00:29:51.410
And then as we refresh this,
Refresh, Refresh, Refresh,

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:52.640
Refresh--

00:29:52.640 --> 00:29:54.110
oh, you know what?

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:58.596
I need to use the
Shift, because caching.

00:29:58.596 --> 00:29:59.095
Maybe.

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:05.660
Let's try closing it
all the way, click.

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:08.737
See how smart I am
about web stuff?

00:30:08.737 --> 00:30:11.232
AUDIENCE: Do you
have to save it?

00:30:11.232 --> 00:30:14.747
SPEAKER 1: Did I not save?

00:30:14.747 --> 00:30:15.580
No, we got it saved.

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:19.340
Probably.

00:30:19.340 --> 00:30:20.155
Sorry.

00:30:20.155 --> 00:30:22.509
That's what you get for
extending your demo on the fly.

00:30:22.509 --> 00:30:24.800
Some of the users are going
to get lemons, some of them

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:27.390
are going to get cherries.

00:30:27.390 --> 00:30:27.890
Thank you.

00:30:30.804 --> 00:30:32.720
So App Engine left
developers focused on code.

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:35.230
We handled the rest.

00:30:35.230 --> 00:30:39.810
Great for web focused workloads,
great for variable load.

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:43.280
We're serving well over
100 billion requests a day

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:45.950
on App Engine for
many, many users.

00:30:45.950 --> 00:30:52.910
So please check out the docs
there and various talks.

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:56.830
And we'll do the
moment for photos.

00:30:56.830 --> 00:30:59.120
And done.

00:30:59.120 --> 00:31:00.860
Cloud Functions-- OK.

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:04.130
So this is functions
of the service.

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:06.080
And functions, in
the abstract sense,

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:08.840
are a thing that
you give some input

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:11.720
and then it gives some output
or has a side effect, right?

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:13.790
And so this is kind
of in some ways kind

00:31:13.790 --> 00:31:16.220
of the purest form of code.

00:31:16.220 --> 00:31:18.560
You just focus on this
specific thing you want to do.

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:21.200
It's got some inputs,
it does something,

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:23.030
and you get some
output, probably.

00:31:26.864 --> 00:31:28.280
It was a little
not obvious to me,

00:31:28.280 --> 00:31:31.070
but one way to reason about
this is it's functions

00:31:31.070 --> 00:31:33.900
that get invoked from something
happening in the world.

00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:35.760
So an event happens.

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:37.560
So we can do events
from a lot of places,

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:42.020
including our Pub/Sub tools
from cloud storage changes,

00:31:42.020 --> 00:31:44.940
from HTTP requests that
come in from somewhere

00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:48.620
if you're using Firebase from
various hooks in Firebase.

00:31:48.620 --> 00:31:51.200
And it is serverless.

00:31:51.200 --> 00:31:53.210
The definition I'm
using of serverless

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:56.200
is anything that--
and App Engine

00:31:56.200 --> 00:31:57.950
fits this as well,
actually-- but anything

00:31:57.950 --> 00:32:02.770
that you're doing
work for money.

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:06.330
So like, you're paying
for a unit of work,

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:09.360
not just reserving a thing
for a certain amount of time.

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:12.116
So you pay for some amount
of work that happens.

00:32:12.116 --> 00:32:15.100
So that's roughly
the definition there.

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:20.210
It has been no JS and JavaScript
kind of for the last year plus.

00:32:20.210 --> 00:32:24.140
We just announced that very,
very soon, Python 3.7 support

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:26.540
will be added, and
you can see continued

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:31.590
release of new
environments soon upcoming,

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:35.230
including something that
is worth checking out,

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:39.050
will be on the blog over
time, but the ability to run

00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:42.290
containers on top
of Cloud Functions,

00:32:42.290 --> 00:32:45.290
so you can kind of wrap
up whatever code you have

00:32:45.290 --> 00:32:46.380
and have that work.

00:32:46.380 --> 00:32:49.340
So that's going to be on
a kind of [? whitelist ?]

00:32:49.340 --> 00:32:51.210
sign up early access program.

00:32:51.210 --> 00:32:54.290
So take a look at
that in the future.

00:32:54.290 --> 00:32:55.880
As the developer,
you're thinking

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:58.700
about events and the function
definition and kind of what

00:32:58.700 --> 00:33:00.480
comes in and out
of the function.

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:01.760
And that's basically it.

00:33:04.270 --> 00:33:06.200
Oops.

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:07.630
Shall we edit slides on the fly?

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:19.080
Look at that-- updated.

00:33:19.080 --> 00:33:23.172
So it is a really good fit for
anything that's event driven.

00:33:23.172 --> 00:33:24.630
When you really
don't want to think

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:26.046
about the runtime
environment, you

00:33:26.046 --> 00:33:28.610
want things to
happen automatically.

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:30.360
Something that may be
a little not obvious

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:33.720
is a lot of data transformation
flows really look like this.

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:35.850
You just kind of feed
them some changes

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:38.280
and it updates other systems
based on those changes.

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.100
So you kind of have the ETL
workloads that a lot of people

00:33:41.100 --> 00:33:41.790
use.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:45.246
And in my mind, it's basically
kind of the glue for the Cloud.

00:33:45.246 --> 00:33:47.370
You know, you want to
connect this API to that API,

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:49.286
and then do this other
thing as a side effect.

00:33:49.286 --> 00:33:51.092
It's a really good fit for that.

00:33:51.092 --> 00:33:52.800
One of the constraints
is you pretty much

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:55.560
have to interact via events.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.890
Can be a HTTP call via events.

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:01.900
Your code is in this
function level granularity.

00:34:01.900 --> 00:34:03.540
So as you get more
functions, you

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:06.560
have to reason about them as
the system gets more complicated

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:09.060
and figure out how you're going
to do logging and monitoring

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:11.639
and things like that.

00:34:11.639 --> 00:34:16.969
And the quick, quick
demo there is missing.

00:34:16.969 --> 00:34:18.389
There we go.

00:34:18.389 --> 00:34:22.520
So I have cleverly deployed
a function already.

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:27.130
It is a very amazing
function that

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:29.860
basically takes this
request in, looks

00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:36.880
in the body of the request
for something called message,

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:39.630
and then returns that.

00:34:39.630 --> 00:34:44.159
So inside of Functions
here, so you can actually

00:34:44.159 --> 00:34:45.750
kind of update them from here.

00:34:48.290 --> 00:34:52.236
This is the URL if you're going
to create an event over HTTP.

00:34:52.236 --> 00:34:53.610
If I click on that
right now, I'm

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:55.989
not actually sending any data.

00:34:55.989 --> 00:35:00.060
So I get the no message
defined response.

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:03.150
And if I want to
send some data--

00:35:03.150 --> 00:35:04.629
copy this example here--

00:35:04.629 --> 00:35:06.420
there's a little kind
of in browser testing

00:35:06.420 --> 00:35:13.560
that you can do and basically
say, hello, lovely Next folks.

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:17.020
And hit Test, and we get a
hello world back and forth.

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:20.000
So I mean, really,
there is no developer--

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:22.016
like dev environment,
it's straight JavaScript

00:35:22.016 --> 00:35:22.890
if you want to do it.

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:24.348
You can also do it
from the command

00:35:24.348 --> 00:35:26.550
line using regular
tools, but you

00:35:26.550 --> 00:35:27.829
can do it all through the web.

00:35:27.829 --> 00:35:30.120
Very straight forward, and
then connect these together.

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:34.060
And there we go.

00:35:34.060 --> 00:35:37.970
So you don't even have
to think about servers.

00:35:37.970 --> 00:35:40.220
That's where the
serverless comes from.

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:41.600
Pay only for what you use.

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:44.390
A very straightforward
developer experience.

00:35:44.390 --> 00:35:51.710
And docs and talks and
photos, 3, 2, 1, gone.

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:55.190
OK, so where should
I run my code?

00:35:55.190 --> 00:35:57.350
Let's get to the actual talk.

00:35:57.350 --> 00:35:58.595
It still depends.

00:35:58.595 --> 00:35:59.960
But on what?

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:02.690
OK, so maybe in listening
to this and the things

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:04.670
I talked about, you
already thought,

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:07.003
OK, I need to check this out,
or I need to look at that.

00:36:07.003 --> 00:36:10.087
So awesome.

00:36:10.087 --> 00:36:11.670
And it depends on
several other things

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:14.820
that I wanted to go into
a little more detail on.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:16.590
This kind of, what
abstraction level

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:20.130
do you want or need to
be working at, what kind

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:22.950
of technical requirements you
have for the language you're

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:26.350
using, the way you talk
to it over the network,

00:36:26.350 --> 00:36:27.930
whether it has
licensing or kernel

00:36:27.930 --> 00:36:29.320
limitations, things like that.

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:32.310
And then your team, your
organization, your colleagues.

00:36:32.310 --> 00:36:35.580
Like what skills
and interests do

00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:37.960
you bring to writing software?

00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:40.320
So in terms of what
you think about,

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:42.930
this is kind of a
summary of the things

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:44.950
I talked about across
the different sections.

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:48.420
So at each level of
abstraction, the way

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:51.550
you think about the code is
actually quite different.

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:53.130
So you may decide
that, actually, we

00:36:53.130 --> 00:36:55.456
want to spend most of our
time focusing on code.

00:36:55.456 --> 00:36:57.330
Or we want to spend most
of our time focusing

00:36:57.330 --> 00:36:59.572
on how the pieces
connect to each other,

00:36:59.572 --> 00:37:00.530
and that sort of thing.

00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:02.300
So that might be
a reason you use

00:37:02.300 --> 00:37:05.220
to start in a certain place.

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:07.140
There are certain
technical requirements

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:10.570
that will kind of pull you
down the stack, if you will.

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:16.240
So if you need a very
specific kernel version,

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:19.210
you're going to go all
the way to Compute Engine.

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:23.290
If you need, you know,
if you want your code

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:25.602
to run pretty much
the same across really

00:37:25.602 --> 00:37:27.310
diverse environments,
you're going to get

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:29.550
pulled at least to
Kubernetes, maybe

00:37:29.550 --> 00:37:33.290
virtual machines,
that sort of thing.

00:37:33.290 --> 00:37:38.644
When you need, you know, to run
very specific languages, that

00:37:38.644 --> 00:37:39.810
might get you an App Engine.

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:42.170
And there's several
other constraints

00:37:42.170 --> 00:37:45.590
as you get into like, research
and docs, that might kind of

00:37:45.590 --> 00:37:47.600
push you one way or the other.

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:52.111
And in some ways, this is
kind of the serverless break,

00:37:52.111 --> 00:37:52.610
if you will.

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:55.640
Like if your team is
primarily developers and want

00:37:55.640 --> 00:37:57.680
to focus on the code
and that sort of thing,

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:01.370
you're probably going to be in
the App Engine and the Cloud

00:38:01.370 --> 00:38:02.960
Functions kind of space.

00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:05.210
If you have good
team integration,

00:38:05.210 --> 00:38:07.310
you've got folks
who are engaging

00:38:07.310 --> 00:38:09.530
in how the systems work
together, and like,

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:12.645
all in on containers, and
there's knobs and parts

00:38:12.645 --> 00:38:14.270
that you really need
to control or want

00:38:14.270 --> 00:38:16.460
to control for your
business needs,

00:38:16.460 --> 00:38:18.990
Kubernetes is going
to be a great fit.

00:38:18.990 --> 00:38:20.630
And if coming over
from existing things

00:38:20.630 --> 00:38:22.970
or, you know, you
want to kind of work

00:38:22.970 --> 00:38:26.360
your way into Cloud step
by step, very deliberately.

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:29.150
Starting with VMs is a
really good place to go.

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:30.980
So Compute Engine.

00:38:30.980 --> 00:38:33.350
If you're running in lots
of different environments,

00:38:33.350 --> 00:38:35.750
this might also do things.

00:38:35.750 --> 00:38:41.360
So this slide's a
little funky, but there

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:43.861
are virtual machines in kind
of almost every environment.

00:38:43.861 --> 00:38:45.860
Like you probably have
them at your own offices,

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:47.450
maybe on your laptop.

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:49.050
All the Cloud
providers have them.

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:51.350
So if your stuff runs
in virtual machines,

00:38:51.350 --> 00:38:53.390
you can run it in
lots of places.

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:56.479
Kubernetes essentially
runs everywhere right now

00:38:56.479 --> 00:38:58.520
and gives you a lot of
insulation from the things

00:38:58.520 --> 00:39:00.600
that do change in the
different environments.

00:39:00.600 --> 00:39:03.440
So it's a really
good choice for that.

00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:07.580
The work that's ongoing in the
community around Envoy, Istio,

00:39:07.580 --> 00:39:11.570
Knative and others,
really are beginning

00:39:11.570 --> 00:39:15.890
to make a really good start
at the kind of platform

00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:18.540
as a service, more like
focusing on the apps and code,

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:20.810
and then inheriting
that insulation

00:39:20.810 --> 00:39:22.280
so that you can
run them anywhere,

00:39:22.280 --> 00:39:23.960
like that you get
from Kubernetes.

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:25.880
So that's worth thinking about.

00:39:25.880 --> 00:39:27.740
And then if you've
got functions that

00:39:27.740 --> 00:39:29.360
are kind of run in JavaScript
or other languages,

00:39:29.360 --> 00:39:30.943
you can probably
move them very easily

00:39:30.943 --> 00:39:33.420
between different
functions' environments.

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:36.812
So kind of things to think
about, like equivalency.

00:39:36.812 --> 00:39:39.020
If you're already kind of
in one of the other places,

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:39.894
look at these pieces.

00:39:39.894 --> 00:39:42.500
Or if you want to
make sure that you

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:45.789
are able to kind of run
in different scenarios,

00:39:45.789 --> 00:39:46.830
think about these pieces.

00:39:46.830 --> 00:39:49.939
So I mentioned
Knative here, right?

00:39:49.939 --> 00:39:50.480
What is that?

00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:52.063
It was in the
announcements yesterday,

00:39:52.063 --> 00:39:54.770
but it's basically
an open source

00:39:54.770 --> 00:39:57.710
project to kind of help
build additional higher

00:39:57.710 --> 00:39:58.430
level primitives.

00:39:58.430 --> 00:40:01.910
Kubernetes is very much kind
of infrastructure, high level

00:40:01.910 --> 00:40:04.400
infrastructure, but
infrastructure project.

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:07.760
And the Knative,
Istio, envoy pieces

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:10.340
are kind of helping us
like, reason at the appli--

00:40:10.340 --> 00:40:13.040
you know, specifically
at the kind of like,

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:15.500
developer focused
code app level,

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:18.440
like all the way up to
almost doing functions.

00:40:18.440 --> 00:40:22.560
So please take a look at that.

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:23.690
Back to here.

00:40:23.690 --> 00:40:28.200
And here's the crux
of the thesis here.

00:40:28.200 --> 00:40:31.170
So why is this no worries?

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:34.950
I think the key thing is, if
you get code into containers,

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:37.710
you can run it in all the
different environments.

00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:40.000
And real systems--

00:40:40.000 --> 00:40:42.990
[INAUDIBLE] say
it-- most systems

00:40:42.990 --> 00:40:45.490
end up running
multiple services,

00:40:45.490 --> 00:40:47.790
multiple different
run times in the end.

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:51.634
They may have some piece of
third party code that only runs

00:40:51.634 --> 00:40:52.800
in a particular environment.

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:55.150
Maybe it needs Windows and
it talks to other stuff,

00:40:55.150 --> 00:40:57.540
and then you have
another part that's

00:40:57.540 --> 00:41:00.489
in functions, because it makes
sense from the data processing

00:41:00.489 --> 00:41:01.780
standpoint, that sort of thing.

00:41:01.780 --> 00:41:04.200
So it's very common
to run multiples

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:06.420
in the same overall system.

00:41:06.420 --> 00:41:10.320
And if you are doing
that, please take a look

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:13.120
at both Pub/Sub
and Cloud storage.

00:41:13.120 --> 00:41:15.660
If you notice you look at
architectural diagrams,

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:17.820
or if you talk to the
Cloud solutions folks,

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:22.980
almost every system will use
Cloud storage and/or Pub/Sub.

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:26.820
Because we actually don't have
local file systems anymore,

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:29.454
or as we're kind of scaling
these things up and down,

00:41:29.454 --> 00:41:30.870
you need to have
good coordination

00:41:30.870 --> 00:41:31.810
between the machines.

00:41:31.810 --> 00:41:34.950
You need places to put
data and get it back out.

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:36.200
So you use a lot of databases.

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:39.240
You'll use Cloud storage
and Pub/Sub a lot.

00:41:39.240 --> 00:41:40.520
So take a look at those.

00:41:40.520 --> 00:41:43.410
They are not the flashiest
bits, but they're everywhere

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:46.300
and super, super useful.

00:41:46.300 --> 00:41:51.120
So if you do get your
code into containers,

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:54.150
you can then move
it relatively easily

00:41:54.150 --> 00:41:57.210
between the different
abstraction levels.

00:41:57.210 --> 00:42:00.090
In particular, for example,
if you write an app

00:42:00.090 --> 00:42:02.160
that you deploy to
App Engine Flex,

00:42:02.160 --> 00:42:03.660
as a side effect
of doing that, it's

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:06.210
actually created a
Docker image, built it,

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:07.764
and written that to a registry.

00:42:07.764 --> 00:42:09.680
So you've got this image
that's sitting there.

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:11.846
And so you can take that
URL and point to Kubernetes

00:42:11.846 --> 00:42:14.580
cluster out of it and say,
run that, and it will run.

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:17.820
You can take that same URL and
put it into the VM creation UI,

00:42:17.820 --> 00:42:20.640
and run that same
image from there.

00:42:20.640 --> 00:42:23.040
So kind of working
down the abstraction

00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:25.770
works like quite smoothly.

00:42:25.770 --> 00:42:28.500
Going up a level, you may need
to do a little manual work

00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:31.090
in some places, but it
should be quite compatible,

00:42:31.090 --> 00:42:33.289
so if you are
already in containers

00:42:33.289 --> 00:42:35.580
that helps move up and down
between these abstractions,

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:37.120
if you decide you need to later.

00:42:37.120 --> 00:42:40.350
So start with these.

00:42:40.350 --> 00:42:43.500
This is the too long,
didn't read version.

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:45.010
Start with these.

00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:48.960
And then as needed, you
can move as necessary.

00:42:48.960 --> 00:42:51.900
So that's why I believe
no worries on that.

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:53.220
We seem to be winding down.

00:42:53.220 --> 00:42:55.914
I will release folks, and I'll
be hanging out to the side

00:42:55.914 --> 00:42:58.080
and then moving out into
the hallway pretty quickly.

00:42:58.080 --> 00:42:59.455
Super happy to
hang out and talk.

00:42:59.455 --> 00:43:01.290
Thank you all for coming.

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:04.340
